esiosis  so we have coverage against all the  pathogens we test for  and some of the pathogens we don't test  for  there are surcharges with each  additional test so this potentially  reduces some of those costs  um which would definitely be an added  benefit over time  i mentioned cmv because it can be a  virus of concern  and once an individual has cmv they  become a carrier and it's not an  issue for most individuals with an  intact immune system  but there are certain populations who  are vulnerable to a more serious disease  including death if they are themselves  negative and are exposed  so right now we have two options cmv  seronegativity  which we call cmb negative and that's  when a donor does not have the igg  antibody to cmv  so we are presuming they have not been  exposed or cnv safe  which means leuco reduced and because  cmv  resides in white blood cells when a  product is leuko reduced the chances of  a white cell carrying cmv is  dramatically reduced  the data have shown these two to be  quite similar in their ability to  prevent cmva  transmission via blood and you can see  here  down below when one might be preferred  over the other  but because pathogen reduction  technology has the ability to act on cnb  this really precludes the need for  either of these  that doesn't mean that these are going  away but again it just  provides an additional layer of safety  a big appeal of prt is that pathogen  reduction in technology preemptively  targets  emerging pathogens now we have just  experienced the emergence of a novel  virus  which fortunately was not a bloodborne  pathogen but hiv  and hepatitis b were being trans uh  transmitted in blood transfusions back  in the 80s  before we even knew what they were or  how to test for them  another bloodborne virus that emerged in  the last decade was zika virus  and we had to go through a period of  figuring out how best to screen for it  whether whether it was developing the  right tests asking the right screening  questions  and this is a process that we have to go  through every time a new virus appears  so the fda released a guidance requiring  that zika virus transmission be  mitigated  and offered two options one was to  perform nucleic acid testing  and the other was to apply a pathogen  reduction technology to the blood  so we are seeing the fda supporting prt  as a viable solution to more than just  bacterial contamination  now interestingly the fda withdrew this  guidance  on may 12 stating it really wasn't  wasn't a relevant transfusion  transmitted infection anymore  but it does give an example of how pr  is assisting improving the safety of  blood supply  so until a pathogen is determined no  longer to no longer pose a threat  or an effective cure or vaccine is found  pr can provide a protective measure for  patients  along with providing savings and testing  and blood processing expenses  speaking of re-emerging pathogens this  is from the cdc website showing  first on the left how effective this  country had been  at decreasing the rate of syphilis since  the 1940s  and unfortunately on the right showing  how in the last decade we have been  seeing a significant uptick in cases  and syphilis is something that we do  test for in  the blood and these are statistics from  the cdc website  website showing a dramatic increase in  stds across the board since 2015.  um blood collectors only test for  syphilis which has increased by 74  percent  and what's important to note here is  that all stds uh  increase the rate of hiv transmission so  of course we continue to test for  syphilis and hiv  but it's reassuring to know that both of  these pathogens  are inactivated by pathogen reduction  technology so remember that prt targets  anything with dna  which means prt is also affected against  t lymphocytes  so this makes prt a viable alternative  uh to gamma or radiation for for the  prevention  of th vhd or transfusion-associated  graft versus host disease and the abb  has a standard describing that blood  banks must have a policy to prevent the  hd  and it includes either irradiation  or pathogen reduction as effective  methods  to achieve this prt actually inactivates  lymphocytes  more effectively than traditional  irradiation you can see  in this top graphic that gamma  irradiation disables dna replication  by causing strand breaks at a rate of 1  in 37  000 base pairs while thorolin  with uva activation induces addict  formation in a rate of  1 in 83 base pairs and this technology  has been fda approved for the prevention  of th bhd  so why is this important thvhd is a very  serious consequence  of blood transfusion in certain  susceptible individuals and it can be  fatal we have over on the right the  orange triangle  represents the blood recipient's tissue  and the yellow  pacman cell represents the lymphocyte or  white blood cell in the donor blood  because it's a white cell and its job is  to attack foreign tissue  it still does that even when it's in the  recipient's body  usually this is not a problem because  the number of recipient white cells  far outnumber the donor white cells and  there is an appropriate counter attack  which you see on the bottom left in the  case of an immunocompromised individual  who is unable to mount that counter  attack the recipient  tissues are vulnerable th bhd is rare  uh has it occurs in about one in a  million transfusions but it has a  mortality in the range of  90 to 100 percent so this is something  that  is very important for transfusion  services to  to prevent this was a small study that  looked at transfusing platelets to  hematopoietic stem cell transplant  patients  who are very immune compromised because  their whole bone marrow is being wiped  out  intentionally and the and they propose  that that pathogen reduction  uh technology could be considered a  replacement for  leukocyte reduction bacterial culture  screening  cmv serology and gamma irradiation  with the additional potential benefits  of course of reducing the cost  due to elimination of all of these  otherwise required  modifications all while reducing the  risk of transfusion-transmitted  infections so just a quick summary  pr t is fda approved and also meets the  aadb standards for bacterial  uh the mitigation of bacterial  contamination  it can be used to replace the  need for serologic testing for cmv it  achieves  lymphocyte inactivation for the  prevention of thg vhd  effectively targets bloodborne protozoa  and spirochetes known to be transmitted  via blood transfusion  and has the potential to preemptively  target any yet unknown emerging  pathogens  so some implications for  blood suppliers is that it's  unidirectional processing  it we don't have to go back to it once  once it just moves forward we can ship  it out  um there are guard band requirements  that dictate volume and concentration  um but with pre-collection and  manufacturing strategies to optimize  uh there are no foreseeable losses due  to the pr process  for implications for hospitals and  transfusion services  they are transfusion ready they provide  the freshest platelet earliest in its  shelf life  and they are currently available only as  a five-day product  with seven days anticipating anticipated  pending fda approval and they replace a  number of needs like irradiation  and cmv seronegativity which has the  potential to enhance availability due to  decreased product segmentation  and potentially decreased costs  i know i'm almost at time here here's a  summary of  options uh that i showed you before  just to refresh your memory and  just to walk you through from the blood  perspective let's supply our perspective  why we chose what we chose  um pathogen reduction platelets are our  primary  uh offering because of all of the  benefits that i just described namely  transfusion ready  and delivering the freshest product and  and the  broad spectrum protection against uh  pathogens beyond bact